<%# The FAQ for machine readers, reached as /faq.md or by negotiating
    `Accept: text/markdown` — see PagesController#faq.

    UNLIKE home.md.erb, THIS FILE HOLDS NO PROSE OF ITS OWN. Both renderings
    walk Seo::Faq, so there is no second copy of an answer to keep in step with
    the first, and none of the claims in docs/privacy/claims.md can be
    accidentally softened in one format and not the other. home.md.erb carries a
    comment asking a future editor to remember to update the other copy; this is
    what that comment would prefer to be.

    MIND THE BLANK LINES. Rails renders ERB through Erubi with trimming on, so a
    line holding nothing but a scriptlet tag is removed along with its newline —
    which is what you want in HTML and silently destroys a markdown document.
    Paragraphs written one per line with a loop tag between them arrive with no
    blank line between them, and markdown then runs the lot together into a
    single paragraph. That is why an answer is emitted as one join on a double
    newline rather than iterated, and why the blank lines below are literal and
    load-bearing.

    Note also that an ERB comment ends at the first closing delimiter inside it,
    which is why this one describes those tags in words rather than showing one.
    The same footgun is documented in crawlers/sitemap.xml.erb, where it has to
    be, because there the stray output would land before the XML declaration. -%>
# Tastatur — frequently asked questions

> Straight answers about consent banners, GDPR, what happens to visitor IP addresses, why cookieless visitor counts differ from Google Analytics, and when you need a data processing agreement.

Where the honest answer is "it depends" or "no", it says so.
<% @entries.each do |entry| -%>

## <%= entry.question %>

<%= entry.answer.join("\n\n") %>
<% if entry.links.any? -%>

<%= entry.links.map { |link| "[#{link.label}](#{public_send(link.route)})" }.join(" · ") %>
<% end -%>
<% end -%>

